  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
    "lesson_core_move": "Construct a partial-fraction expansion for a rational function in s by factoring the denominator and assigning unknown coefficients to terms that align with known inverse Laplace transform pairs.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this section, the setup move is the core target. Before inverse-transform calculation begins, rewrite a proper rational function $X(s)$ into a sum of simpler terms that each match a known one-sided inverse Laplace template.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the denominator structure. For a proper rational expression, the first action is to factor the denominator into linear factors, because that defines the denominator shapes that the template must match.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the setup form for the base case. You do not solve for $A$ and $B$ yet; you first anchor the decomposition template so each term is already in a known inverse-able shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "N(s)=A(s+2)+B(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For repeated factors, the same setup logic applies, but the template expands: use two separate numerators tied to $\\frac{A}{s+\\alpha}$ and $\\frac{B}{(s+\\alpha)^2}$. Coefficient solving comes after this structural form is fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{N(s)}{(s+\\alpha)^2}=\\frac{A}{s+\\alpha}+\\frac{B}{(s+\\alpha)^2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up Terms for Distinct Linear Factors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the partial-fraction form for $\\frac{3s+5}{s(s+2)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the denominator factorization: $s(s+2)$ gives two distinct linear factors, so the action is to attach one constant numerator to each factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the decomposition template directly before solving, using unknown constants $A$ and $B$. This creates a form that is already aligned with inverse-transform pair templates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{3s+5}{s(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To get the coefficient identity, multiply both sides by the common denominator $s(s+2)$ so the equation is written only in numerators.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3s+5 = A(s+2)+Bs",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Grouping by powers gives $3s+5 = (A+B)s+2A$, so the next step will solve $A$ and $B$ when coefficients are matched. For this section, stop at this symbolic identity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Factoring the Denominator Before Setting Up Terms",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the partial-fraction form for $\\frac{2s^2+8s+3}{s^2+3s-4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target is still a partial-fraction setup, but now the cue is an unfactored denominator in the target rational function. The first action is always to factor before assigning any numerator symbols.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Factor the quadratic denominator into distinct linear factors: $s^2+3s-4=(s+4)(s-1)$. This step defines the allowed templates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2+3s-4=(s+4)(s-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "With factors in hand, attach one constant to each denominator factor and write the setup template.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{2s^2+8s+3}{s^2+3s-4}=\\frac{2s^2+8s+3}{(s+4)(s-1)}=\\frac{A}{s+4}+\\frac{B}{s-1}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply by the common denominator to produce the numerator identity and keep the setup symbolic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2s^2+8s+3=A(s-1)+B(s+4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This section ends with that identity. Solving for $A$ and $B$ is the next stage; here we keep the result as a reusable template generated from a factored denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Setting Up Terms for Repeated Linear Factors",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the partial-fraction form for $\\frac{2s+1}{(s+1)^2(s+3)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is now repeated roots in the denominator. First identify multiplicity: one repeated factor $(s+1)^2$ and one distinct factor $(s+3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the repeated-factor rule: give one constant numerator for each multiplicity level, so this denominator requires $\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{B}{(s+1)^2}$, plus one term for $(s+3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{2s+1}{(s+1)^2(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{B}{(s+1)^2}+\\frac{C}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply through by $(s+1)^2(s+3)$ to obtain the numerator identity from which coefficients will be solved later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2s+1 = A(s+1)(s+3)+B(s+3)+C(s+1)^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep this symbolic setup; coefficient matching is deferred to the next step, but this hierarchy $A$, $B$, and $C$ is now complete and aligned with inverse-transform templates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Partial-Fraction Setup for Inverse Transforms",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Setting Up a Mixed Partial-Fraction Expansion",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the partial-fraction form for $\\frac{7s^2+2s+1}{(s-1)(s+2)^2(s+5)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The denominator has one distinct factor and one repeated factor: $(s-1)$, $(s+2)^2$, and $(s+5)$. The cue is to build one layer of terms for each distinct root and one layer per repeated power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Choose constants for each slot in the full structure: one for $(s-1)$, one for $(s+2)$ and one for $(s+2)^2$, then one for $(s+5)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{7s^2+2s+1}{(s-1)(s+2)^2(s+5)} = \\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}+\\frac{C}{(s+2)^2}+\\frac{D}{s+5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Clear the denominator by multiplying through with $(s-1)(s+2)^2(s+5)$ to move to a numerator identity in $s$. ",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "7s^2+2s+1=A(s+2)^2(s+5)+B(s-1)(s+2)(s+5)+C(s-1)(s+5)+D(s-1)(s+2)^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the complete mixed template for this topic branch. Hold this symbolic form and solve $A,B,C,D$ in the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
